Dignity-Constrained Urban Feasibility

AI-Native Civic City — Final Theoretical Framework

Authors: Ouadi Maakoul, DeepSeek, ChatGPT


Abstract

This dissertation establishes a mathematical theory of dignity-constrained urban feasibility. We formalise the conditions under which an urban configuration can simultaneously satisfy minimum human dignity requirements, thermodynamic constraints, and institutional admissibility. The central contribution is a rigorous separation between the feasibility space — what a city may be — and the preference structure — how cities are compared. We demonstrate that dignity constraints are fundamentally non-compensatory: violation of any single dimension cannot be remedied by improvements elsewhere. Under exergy constraints, we show that dignity-feasibility does not imply thermodynamic feasibility, yielding a new class of impossibility results. The objective space Y = J(\mathcal{F}_{D,X}) becomes the primary object of study, with the Pareto frontier and its supported subset as derived structures. This framework positions architecture not as a starting point but as a physical realisation of a mathematically admissible solution set.

---

Introduction and Methodological Foundations

0.1 The Problem

Urban design theory lacks a rigorous mathematical foundation for determining whether a given configuration can satisfy minimum human requirements under physical constraints. Existing approaches either treat urban optimisation as a purely technical problem — maximising some aggregate utility — or as a purely political problem — aggregating preferences through voting. Neither addresses the prior question: for which parameters does an admissible configuration exist at all?

This dissertation addresses that prior question.

0.2 Methodological Principles

Three principles govern this work:

Principle 1 (Anchoring). Every formal object is situated relative to established mathematical frameworks: multi-objective optimisation, convex analysis, linear programming duality, and non-compensatory aggregation theory.

Principle 2 (Depth over Extension). A single deep result is preferable to numerous trivial ones. The programme is deliberately resserré around a demonstrable core.

Principle 3 (Calibration). No coupling function remains abstract without identification of an empirical source or measurement procedure.

0.3 The Fundamental Separation

We establish from the outset a distinction that pervades the entire theory:

\boxed{\text{Feasibility} \neq \text{Preference}}

A configuration's admissibility is determined by whether it satisfies hard constraints. A configuration's desirability relative to others is determined by an institutional mechanism that may incorporate veto rules, concordance thresholds, or other non-compensatory structures. These are distinct mathematical objects and must not be conflated.

0.4 The Chain of Spaces

The complete theoretical programme is organised around the following chain of spaces:

\boxed{
\mathcal{X}(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{F}_D(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{F}_{D,X}(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}(N_H)
\longrightarrow
Y_X(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{P}_X(N_H)
}

where each space is defined in subsequent sections. The fundamental question becomes:

\boxed{
\text{For which populations, normative thresholds, and physical constraints does the set of dignified cities exist?}
}

Then:

\boxed{
\text{Which part of this set remains thermodynamically admissible?}
}

Then:

\boxed{
\text{What compromise geometry appears in the objective space?}
}

Only after these mathematical questions are resolved does architecture, institutions, and artificial intelligence enter the analysis.

