Part I: Mathematical Foundations

Preliminaries and Notation

1.1 Spaces

Let \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m denote the physical configuration space: the set of all physically representable urban configurations. A generic element x \in \mathcal{X} encodes the spatial, volumetric, and functional parameters of a city.

Let J : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}^7 denote the objective map, where:

J(x) = \big(E(x),\, W(x),\, L(x),\, C(x),\, \mathrm{Risk}(x),\, -Q_H(x),\, -B(x)\big),

with components representing energy, water, land, connectivity, risk, thermal quality, and civic bonding respectively.

1.2 Population Variable

Since several dignity constraints are defined per person, we introduce the population variable:

N_H \in \mathbb{R}_{++}.

The physical configuration space becomes population-parameterised:

\mathcal{X}(N_H) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m.

All dignity dimensions are henceforth understood as functions of both configuration and population:

d_k = d_k(x, N_H), \qquad k \in \mathcal{K}.

1.3 Dignity Dimensions

Let \mathcal{K} = \{P, S, A, M, N, T, C\} index the seven dignity dimensions:

Symbol Dimension Unit
P Private space per person m²/person
S Security index dimensionless
A Accessibility to essential services dimensionless
M Mobility range km
N Access to nature m²/person
T Thermal comfort index dimensionless
C Civic connectivity dimensionless

1.4 Normative Thresholds and Capabilities Anchoring

Each raw dimension d_k is normalised by a normative threshold d_k^{\min} > 0:

z_k(x, N_H) = \frac{d_k(x, N_H)}{d_k^{\min}}, \qquad k \in \mathcal{K}.

The vector \theta = (d_1^{\min}, \ldots, d_7^{\min}) \in \mathbb{R}^7_{++} constitutes the normative parameter vector.

Anchoring in the Capabilities Approach. The normative thresholds are not arbitrary. They are derived from Nussbaum's central human capabilities, which specify that respect for human dignity requires citizens to be placed above an ample threshold of capability in each of ten central areas. The mapping is as follows:

Dignity Dimension Nussbaum Capability
P (private space) Bodily Integrity + Affiliation
S (security) Life + Bodily Integrity
A (accessibility) Practical Reason + Affiliation
M (mobility) Bodily Integrity + Senses, Imagination, and Thought
N (nature access) Other Species + Play
T (thermal comfort) Bodily Health
C (civic connectivity) Affiliation + Control over One's Environment

This mapping converts an arbitrary list into a theoretically grounded set and provides a principled basis for arguing that threshold violation in any dimension constitutes a dignity violation.

1.5 The Feasibility Correspondence

Since thresholds will remain contested even within a capabilities framework, we characterise feasibility as a function of \theta, not for a single \theta. Define the feasibility correspondence:

\Theta \mapsto \mathcal{F}_\theta(N_H), \qquad \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^7_{++}.

The research programme identifies the critical region \partial\Theta where \mathcal{F}_\theta(N_H) transitions from non-empty to empty. This transforms the threshold problem from a political liability into a mathematical object of study.

