Part II: Feasibility and Preference — The Fundamental Separation

2.1 Critique of Conflated Formulations

A common error in multi-criteria decision analysis is to conflate the set of feasible alternatives with the preference relation used to rank them. This conflation produces formulations such as:

\mathcal{F}_E(\theta, v) = \{x : z_k(x) \geq \theta_k \; \forall k\} \cap \{x : \#\{k : z_k(x) < v_k\} = 0\}.

For v_k > \theta_k, the second condition implies z_k(x) \geq v_k for all k, reducing to:

\mathcal{F}_E(\theta, v) = \{x : z_k(x) \geq v_k \; \forall k\}.

The proposed "partial veto" has mathematically vanished. It is merely a set of higher thresholds, with no compensation mechanism whatsoever.

2.2 The Correct Separation

We therefore define two distinct objects.

Definition 1 (Feasibility). The dignity-feasible set is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_\theta(N_H) = \big\{x \in \mathcal{X}(N_H) : z_k(x, N_H) \geq 1 \;\; \forall k \in \mathcal{K}\big\}.
}

This defines what a city may be.

Definition 2 (Preference). A preference structure is a binary relation \succsim_\iota on \mathcal{F}_\theta(N_H), parameterised by an institutional mechanism \iota. This relation may be a linear scalarisation, a concordance-discordance rule (ELECTRE-type), or any other procedure satisfying specified axioms. This defines how cities are compared.

2.3 Principle of Non-Compensation

\boxed{\text{A city cannot compensate for violation of a human minimum by superior performance elsewhere.}}

This principle is encoded in the conjunctive structure of \mathcal{F}_\theta: all constraints must be satisfied simultaneously. Once feasibility is established, different architectures may be compared by different preference procedures.

2.4 The Non-Compensation Impossibility

A fundamental result from aggregation theory: no indicator can be simultaneously aggregated, sensitive, anti-catastrophic, and non-compensatory. If the institutional mechanism is required to be non-compensatory, it cannot also be a complete weak order under mild assumptions. The only such procedure is the lexicographic order, which is pathologically insensitive to intensity.

Consequence. The institutional mechanism may legitimately leave alternatives incomparable. The theory studies the structure of the incomparability relation and its implications for decision-making.

