Part IV: The Geometry of Pareto Support

4.1 Definitions

Definition 3. Let J : \mathcal{F}_D \to \mathbb{R}^7 and let Y = J(\mathcal{F}_D). We define the following classes of Pareto-optimal points:

\mathcal{P}_{+} = \left\{ y \in \mathcal{P} : \exists w \gg 0, \; y \in \arg\min_{y' \in Y} w^\top y' \right\},

\mathcal{P}_{\geq 0} = \left\{ y \in \mathcal{P} : \exists w \geq 0, \; w \neq 0, \; y \in \arg\min_{y' \in Y} w^\top y' \right\}.

The set of non-supported Pareto points is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{P}_{\text{ns}} = \mathcal{P} \setminus \mathcal{P}_{\geq 0}.
}

4.2 Hierarchy

\boxed{
\mathcal{P}_{+} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_{\geq 0} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_{\text{Pareto}}.
}

4.3 The Linear Scalarisation Limitation

Theorem 1 (Linear Scalarisation Limitation). Linear scalarisation with non-negative weights cannot recover non-supported Pareto points.

Proof. If y minimises w \cdot y' for some w \in \mathbb{R}^7_+, then y \in \mathcal{P}_{\geq 0} by definition. Hence y \notin \mathcal{P}_{\text{ns}}. \square

Corollary. If \mathcal{P}_{\text{ns}} \neq \varnothing, then no linear aggregation of objectives can reach the corresponding configurations.

Remark. This is a statement about a specific aggregation mechanism. It does not preclude other decision procedures from selecting non-supported points.

4.4 Non-Convexity and Non-Support

Critical distinction. The non-convexity of \mathcal{F}_D does not, by itself, imply \mathcal{P}_{\text{ns}} \neq \varnothing. The relevant object is the objective image:

\boxed{
Y = J(\mathcal{F}_D) \text{ non-convex} \;\Longrightarrow\; \text{possibility of non-supported Pareto points.}
}

However, the implication is not reversible: it is possible that \mathcal{F}_D is non-convex while J(\mathcal{F}_D) is convex. In that case, architectural non-convexity does not produce non-supported Pareto points.

The object of study is therefore the triple:

\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_D \;\longrightarrow\; Y = J(\mathcal{F}_D) \;\longrightarrow\; \mathcal{P}_{\text{Pareto}}.
}

4.5 Tchebycheff Scalarisation as Recovery Method

While linear scalarisation cannot recover non-supported Pareto points, the augmented Tchebycheff scalarisation can recover any Pareto point, including non-supported ones, when the ideal point is known:

\min_{x \in \mathcal{F}_D} \max_{k} \left[ w_k (J_k(x) - z_k^*) \right] + \rho \sum_k (J_k(x) - z_k^*),

where z_k^* are the ideal objective values and \rho > 0 is an augmentation parameter. This scalarisation is capable of exploring the entire Pareto front by traversing all preference vectors within the preference simplex, ensuring that both convex and concave regions are covered. It is particularly effective for non-convex Pareto fronts where weighted-sum methods fail.

Computational caveat. Tchebycheff scalarisation requires knowledge of the ideal point z^*, which depends on solving individual single-objective problems. For the dignity-constrained framework, this means solving seven separate feasibility problems. This is computationally tractable but should be acknowledged as a cost.

