Part VII: Exergy as Constitutive Constraint

7.1 Exergy Destruction

Let X_{\text{dest}} : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}_+ denote exergy destruction. For a thermodynamic system, exergy destruction is related to entropy generation by:

X_{\text{dest}} = T_0 S_{\text{gen}},

where T_0 is the reference temperature and S_{\text{gen}} is the entropy generation rate.

Critical distinction. Exergy consumption \neq exergy destruction. Exergy consumption refers to the input of exergy resources; exergy destruction refers to the irreversible loss of exergy quality within the system.

7.2 Component Decomposition

The entropy generation is decomposed into subsystems:

S_{\text{gen}} = S_{\text{gen}}^{\text{HVAC}} + S_{\text{gen}}^{\text{water}} + S_{\text{gen}}^{\text{mobility}} + S_{\text{gen}}^{\text{building}} + \cdots,

with careful attention to system boundaries.

7.3 Exergy-Constrained Feasibility

The exergy-constrained feasible set is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_{D,X} = \left\{ V : \begin{array}{l} z_k(V) \geq 1, \;\forall k \\ \mathbf{1}^\top V \leq V_{\max} \\ X_{\text{dest}}(V) \leq X_{\max} \end{array} \right\}.
}

7.4 The Impossibility Mechanism

Define:

\Delta \mathcal{F}_X = \mathcal{F}_D \setminus \mathcal{F}_{D,X}.

This is the set of configurations satisfying human dignity requirements but thermodynamically inadmissible.

\boxed{\text{Dignity-feasible} \;\not\Rightarrow\; \text{thermodynamically-feasible}.}

7.5 Objective Image Under Exergy Constraint

Y_X = J(\mathcal{F}_{D,X}).

The Pareto frontier becomes:

\mathcal{P}_{\text{Pareto},X} = \text{NonDominated}(Y_X).

7.6 The Exergy Difference

We define:

\boxed{
\Delta Y_X = Y_D \setminus Y_{D,X}
}

and:

\boxed{
\Delta \mathcal{P}_X = \mathcal{P}_D \setminus \mathcal{P}_{D,X}.
}

This formalises the phenomenon whereby an architecture that is dignity-feasible may disappear entirely when the exergy constraint is imposed, while another architecture, previously dominated, may become Pareto-relevant after the exergy constraint is introduced.

