Part VIII: Architectural and Institutional Consequences

8.1 Architecture as Realisation Mapping

Correction. The formula \text{Architecture} = \pi_{\mathcal{X}}(\mathcal{F}_{D,X}) is mathematically incorrect because \mathcal{F}_{D,X} \subseteq \mathcal{X}, so the canonical projection is the identity. The correct formulation introduces a synthesis mapping:

\boxed{
\mathcal{A} : \mathcal{F}_{D,X} \to \mathcal{G}_{\text{phys}},
}

where \mathcal{G}_{\text{phys}} is the space of built geometries and configurations. A concrete architecture is a physical realisation of an element of the admissible configuration space.

Degeneracy of architectural realisation. This permits the question: can the same urban objective be realised by multiple physically distinct geometries?

\mathcal{A}(x_1) \neq \mathcal{A}(x_2) \quad \text{while} \quad J(x_1) = J(x_2).

This is the architectural degeneracy of the feasibility problem.

8.2 Coherence Constraint

The formula \text{Architecture} = \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{F}_{D,X}) is a necessary condition, not a sufficient one. Many physically admissible configurations are architecturally meaningless. We introduce a coherence constraint:

\mathcal{C}(x) \geq 0,

encoding architectural and urbanistic quality beyond the dignity minima. The feasible set becomes:

\mathcal{F}_{\text{arch}} = \{x \in \mathcal{F}_{D,X} : \mathcal{C}(x) \geq 0\}.

The coherence constraint can be formalised using shape grammar theory or space syntax.

8.3 Institutional Admissibility

Definition 4. Let \mathcal{U}(x) denote the set of physically realisable actions at configuration x. An institution \iota defines institutionally admissible actions:

\boxed{
\mathcal{U}_\iota(x) \subseteq \mathcal{U}(x).
}

The institutionally admissible feasible set is:

\boxed{
\mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota} = \{x \in \mathcal{F}_{D,X} : \mathcal{U}_\iota(x) \neq \varnothing\}.
}

The preference relation \succsim_\iota then acts on \mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}.

8.4 The Complete Chain

\boxed{
\mathcal{X}(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{F}_D(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{F}_{D,X}(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}(N_H)
\longrightarrow
Y_X(N_H)
\longrightarrow
\mathcal{P}_X(N_H)
}

8.5 The Political Constitution of \iota

Institutions are not preference relations; they are power structures. The choice of \iota is a political choice, not a mathematical one. The mathematical framework can analyse the consequences of different \iota, but it cannot determine which \iota is legitimate. This is a normative question that requires democratic deliberation, not mathematical derivation. The theory positions itself as a tool for informed deliberation, not as a substitute for it.

