Part X: Conclusion

This dissertation establishes a mathematical theory of dignity-constrained urban feasibility. The central contributions are:

1. The feasibility-preference separation: A rigorous distinction between what a city may be and how cities are compared.
2. The chain of spaces: Physical space \mathcal{X}(N_H), feasible space \mathcal{F}_D(N_H), exergy-constrained feasible space \mathcal{F}_{D,X}(N_H), institutionally admissible space \mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}(N_H), objective space Y_X(N_H), and Pareto frontier \mathcal{P}_X(N_H).
3. The non-compensation principle: Dignity constraints are conjunctive; violation of any dimension cannot be remedied elsewhere.
4. The exergy impossibility mechanism: Dignity-feasibility does not imply thermodynamic feasibility.
5. The non-supported Pareto problem: Linear scalarisation cannot recover all Pareto-optimal configurations; Tchebycheff scalarisation provides a recovery method.
6. Population scaling: The maximum sustainable population N_{\max} is a mathematical consequence of dignity constraints and physical coupling.

The programme proceeds from foundations to applications:

\boxed{
\text{Mathematics} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Architecture} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Institutions} \;\longrightarrow\; \text{Scale.}
}

The next phase concerns the quadratic case n = 3 and the convexity properties of the objective image. The fundamental question remains:

\boxed{
\text{For which populations, normative thresholds, and physical constraints does the set of dignified cities exist?}
}

And then:

\boxed{
\text{Which part of this set remains thermodynamically admissible?}
}

And finally:

\boxed{
\text{What compromise geometry appears in the objective space?}
}

Only after these mathematical questions are resolved do architecture, institutions, and artificial intelligence enter the analysis.

