Appendix A: Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Farkas' Lemma — Full Derivation for the Linear Feasibility Problem

We provide a complete derivation of the Farkas certificate used in Theorem 2A.

Lemma (Farkas). Let M \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times n} and r \in \mathbb{R}^p. Exactly one of the following holds:

1. There exists V \in \mathbb{R}^n with MV \geq r and V \geq 0.
2. There exists y \in \mathbb{R}^p with y \geq 0, M^\top y \leq 0, and r^\top y > 0.

Application to the dignity feasibility problem. Our system is:

AV \geq b, \quad -\mathbf{1}^\top V \geq -V_{\max}, \quad V \geq 0.

We set:

M = \begin{pmatrix} A \\ -\mathbf{1}^\top \end{pmatrix}, \qquad r = \begin{pmatrix} b \\ -V_{\max} \end{pmatrix}.

The dual certificate is y = (\mu, \nu) \in \mathbb{R}^{K+1}_+, and the two conditions of Farkas become:

M^\top y = A^\top \mu - \nu \mathbf{1} \leq 0,

r^\top y = b^\top \mu - V_{\max} \nu > 0.

Translating back: \mathcal{F}_D \neq \varnothing if and only if there is no (\mu, \nu) \geq 0 with \mu \neq 0 or \nu > 0 such that:

\sum_k \mu_k a_k - \nu \mathbf{1} \leq 0, \qquad \sum_k \mu_k b_k - \nu V_{\max} > 0.

Remark on sign conventions. The original formulation in the main text uses b_k - 1 because we wrote a_k^\top V \geq 1 - b_k. The two forms are equivalent under the substitution b_k \leftarrow b_k - 1.

A.2 Convex Feasibility via Separation

Theorem A.1. Let g_k : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}, k = 1, \ldots, K, be convex functions. Then \mathcal{F}_D = \{V : g_k(V) \leq 0 \; \forall k\} \neq \varnothing if and only if:

\inf_{V} \max_k g_k(V) \leq 0.

Proof. The function G(V) = \max_k g_k(V) is convex as the maximum of convex functions. Its sublevel set \{V : G(V) \leq 0\} equals \mathcal{F}_D. Thus \mathcal{F}_D \neq \varnothing iff \inf_V G(V) \leq 0. \square

Dual formulation. Introducing multipliers \mu_k \geq 0 for each constraint, the Lagrangian is:

\mathcal{L}(V, \mu) = \sum_k \mu_k g_k(V), \qquad \mu \in \Delta^K.

The dual function is:

d(\mu) = \inf_V \sum_k \mu_k g_k(V).

Under Slater's condition (there exists V_0 with g_k(V_0) < 0 for all k), strong duality holds:

\inf_V \max_k g_k(V) = \max_{\mu \in \Delta^K} \inf_V \sum_k \mu_k g_k(V).

Thus \mathcal{F}_D \neq \varnothing iff \max_\mu d(\mu) \leq 0.

A.3 Proof of the 2D Impossibility Condition

Proposition A.2. In the 2D linear model with z_P = \alpha \lambda and z_N = \beta(1 - \lambda), the feasible set \mathcal{F}_D \neq \varnothing iff:

\frac{1}{\alpha} + \frac{1}{\beta} \leq 1.

Proof. The constraints z_P \geq 1 and z_N \geq 1 become:

\alpha \lambda \geq 1 \iff \lambda \geq \frac{1}{\alpha},

\beta(1 - \lambda) \geq 1 \iff \lambda \leq 1 - \frac{1}{\beta}.

A feasible \lambda \in [0,1] exists iff the lower bound does not exceed the upper bound:

\frac{1}{\alpha} \leq 1 - \frac{1}{\beta} \iff \frac{1}{\alpha} + \frac{1}{\beta} \leq 1.

Substituting \alpha = p_0 / p^{\min} and \beta = n_0 / n^{\min} gives the stated condition. \square

A.4 Non-Supported Pareto Point Construction

Theorem A.3 (Existence of Non-Supported Pareto Points). Let Y \subset \mathbb{R}^2 be a non-convex, compact set whose Pareto frontier contains a point y^* that lies strictly inside the convex hull of the dominated region. Then y^* \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{ns}}.

Proof sketch. Suppose y^* \in \mathcal{P}_{\geq 0}. Then there exists w \geq 0 with w^\top y^* \leq w^\top y for all y \in Y. This means the hyperplane \{y : w^\top y = w^\top y^*\} supports Y at y^*. But if y^* lies strictly inside the convex hull of Y, no such supporting hyperplane with non-negative normal exists. Contradiction. Hence y^* \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{ns}}. \square

Explicit example. Let Y = \{(0,1), (1,0)\} \cup \{(t, t) : t \in [0.4, 0.6]\}. The point (0.5, 0.5) is Pareto-optimal but not supported: any w \geq 0 that minimises at (0.5, 0.5) would also minimise at (0,0), which is not in Y. The point (0.5, 0.5) lies in a "dent" of the Pareto frontier.

A.5 Tchebycheff Scalarisation — Convergence Guarantee

Theorem A.4. Let Y \subset \mathbb{R}^p be a compact set and let z^* \in \mathbb{R}^p be its ideal point. For every y \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{Pareto}}, there exists w \in \mathbb{R}^p_+ \setminus \{0\} such that y is a unique minimiser of:

\max_{k} \left[ w_k (y_k - z_k^*) \right] + \rho \sum_k w_k (y_k - z_k^*)

for any \rho > 0 sufficiently small.

Proof. See Steuer (1986), Theorem 4.5. The augmentation term \rho \sum_k w_k (y_k - z_k^*) ensures uniqueness by eliminating ties that would otherwise occur at non-supported points. \square

