Appendix D: Empirical Calibration Protocols

D.1 Dignity Threshold Calibration

Each dignity threshold d_k^{\min} is calibrated through a three-stage process:

Stage 1: Normative derivation. Using Nussbaum's capabilities as the philosophical foundation, derive a range of plausible thresholds from the literature.

Stage 2: Expert elicitation. Convene a panel of 12–20 experts (architects, urban planners, public health specialists, ethicists, community representatives). Use the Delphi method to converge on a consensus range.

Stage 3: Participatory validation. Conduct deliberative workshops in 5–10 communities representing diverse socio-economic and cultural contexts. Present the derived thresholds and solicit feedback on their adequacy and legitimacy.

The output is a threshold interval [d_k^{\min,\text{low}}, d_k^{\min,\text{high}}] for each dimension, which defines the critical region \partial\Theta.

D.2 Exergy Destruction Measurement

Exergy destruction is measured at three scales:

Building scale. For each building type, compute:

X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{building}} = T_0 \int_0^\tau \dot{S}_{\text{gen}}(t) \, dt

where \dot{S}_{\text{gen}} is the entropy generation rate, computed from measured temperature, pressure, and mass flow data.

District scale. Aggregate building-scale values and add distribution losses:

X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{district}} = \sum_i X_{\text{dest},i}^{\text{building}} + X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{distribution}}.

City scale. Add transport, water, and waste subsystems:

X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{city}} = X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{district}} + X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{transport}} + X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{water}} + X_{\text{dest}}^{\text{waste}}.

D.3 Civic Connectivity Measurement

Civic connectivity is measured through a composite index:

C = w_1 C_{\text{assoc}} + w_2 C_{\text{gov}} + w_3 C_{\text{interact}} + w_4 C_{\text{shared}},

where:

· C_{\text{assoc}}: membership rate in voluntary associations
· C_{\text{gov}}: participation rate in local governance
· C_{\text{interact}}: frequency of neighbourly interaction (survey)
· C_{\text{shared}}: utilisation rate of shared facilities

Weights are calibrated via factor analysis on pilot data.

D.4 Population Scaling Calibration

The maximum sustainable population N_{\max} is calibrated by:

1. Fixing the physical volume budget V_{\max}.
2. Varying N_H and computing \mathcal{F}_D(N_H).
3. Identifying the critical N_H^* at which \mathcal{F}_D(N_H) becomes empty.
4. Repeating for different threshold vectors \theta to map the feasibility boundary.

