Appendix F: Institutional Mechanism Design

F.1 The Institutional Selection Problem

Given the feasible set \mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}, the institutional mechanism selects a configuration. We formalise this as a social choice problem under constraints.

Definition F.1. An institutional mechanism is a mapping:

\iota : \prod_{i=1}^{n} \succsim_i \to \mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}

from a profile of individual preferences to a selected configuration.

F.2 Axioms

We propose the following axioms for institutional mechanisms:

Axiom 1 (Feasibility). The selected configuration must belong to \mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}.

Axiom 2 (Pareto Efficiency). If all individuals prefer x to y, then y is not selected when x is available.

Axiom 3 (Non-Compensation). No individual's dignity threshold may be violated in the selected configuration.

Axiom 4 (Anonymity). The mechanism treats all individuals symmetrically.

Axiom 5 (Strategy-Proofness). Truthful revelation of preferences is a dominant strategy.

F.3 Impossibility Result

Theorem F.2. No institutional mechanism satisfies Axioms 1–5 simultaneously when |\mathcal{F}_{D,X,\iota}| \geq 3 and n \geq 2.

Proof sketch. By the Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem, any strategy-proof mechanism with at least three alternatives is dictatorial. But dictatorship violates Anonymity. Hence no mechanism satisfies all five axioms. \square

Consequence. The institutional layer must relax at least one axiom. The most defensible relaxation is Strategy-Proofness: deliberative democracy accepts that preferences may be strategically revealed, and relies on institutional design (transparency, rotation, deliberation) to mitigate manipulation.

F.4 ELECTRE-Type Outranking

We adopt an ELECTRE-type outranking relation:

x \succsim_\iota y \iff \phi^+(x,y) \geq \lambda \text{ and } \phi^-(x,y) \leq \gamma,

where:

· \phi^+(x,y) is the concordance index (weight of criteria favouring x over y)
· \phi^-(x,y) is the discordance index (weight of criteria opposing x over y)
· \lambda is the concordance threshold
· \gamma is the discordance threshold

Key property. The outranking relation is not complete: there may exist pairs (x,y) with neither x \succsim_\iota y nor y \succsim_\iota x. This incomparability is a feature, not a bug: it reflects genuine value pluralism.

F.5 Deliberative Escalation

When the outranking relation leaves a set of mutually incomparable alternatives, the mechanism escalates to deliberation:

1. Technical deliberation: Experts present the trade-offs in objective space.
2. Citizen deliberation: Randomly selected citizens deliberate on the trade-offs.
3. Institutional decision: The relevant authority selects, with justification.

This three-stage process ensures that incomparability is resolved politically, not mathematically.

