Appendix G: Thermodynamic System Boundaries

G.1 Defining the System

Exergy destruction is only well-defined relative to a system boundary. We define the urban system boundary as:

\partial \mathcal{S} = \partial \mathcal{S}_{\text{physical}} \cup \partial \mathcal{S}_{\text{temporal}} \cup \partial \mathcal{S}_{\text{functional}}.

Physical boundary. The spatial extent of the settlement, including buildings, infrastructure, and managed green space.

Temporal boundary. The life-cycle horizon: construction, operation, and end-of-life.

Functional boundary. The services provided: shelter, mobility, water, food, waste management.

G.2 Exergy Accounting

For each subsystem, we compute:

X_{\text{dest}} = X_{\text{in}} - X_{\text{out}} - X_{\text{stored}},

where:

· X_{\text{in}} is the exergy input (fuels, electricity, materials)
· X_{\text{out}} is the exergy output (products, waste heat)
· X_{\text{stored}} is the exergy stored in the system

G.3 System Boundaries for Specific Subsystems

Subsystem Physical Boundary Temporal Boundary Functional Boundary
HVAC Building envelope Operational life Thermal comfort
Water District Operational life Potable water supply
Mobility City region Vehicle life Passenger-km
Building Structure + envelope 50–100 years Shelter
