K.2 Glossary

Accessibility. The ease with which residents can reach essential services, measured as a composite of proximity, transit frequency, and universal design.

Civic connectivity. The density and quality of social ties, participation in governance, and use of shared facilities.

Coherence constraint. A condition on physical configurations that encodes architectural and urbanistic quality beyond dignity minima.

Dignity dimension. One of seven minimum requirements for human dignity in urban settings: private space, security, accessibility, mobility, nature access, thermal comfort, civic connectivity.

Exergy destruction. The irreversible loss of exergy quality within a system, equal to T_0 S_{\text{gen}}.

Feasibility. The property of a configuration satisfying all hard constraints (dignity minima, volume budget, exergy budget).

Feasibility correspondence. The mapping from threshold vectors to feasible sets.

Institutional mechanism. A procedure for selecting from the feasible set, parameterised by an institution \iota.

Non-compensation. The principle that violation of any dignity dimension cannot be remedied by improvement in another.

Non-supported Pareto point. A Pareto-optimal point that cannot be recovered by linear scalarisation with non-negative weights.

Objective image. The image of the feasible set under the objective map.

Pareto frontier. The set of non-dominated points in the objective image.

Preference. A binary relation on the feasible set used for comparison and selection.

Properly supported Pareto point. A Pareto point that uniquely minimises a linear scalarisation with strictly positive weights.

Supported Pareto point. A Pareto point that minimises a linear scalarisation with non-negative weights.

Tchebycheff scalarisation. A scalarisation method that can recover non-supported Pareto points by minimising a weighted maximum deviation from the ideal point.

Thermal comfort. The condition of mind that expresses satisfaction with the thermal environment, measured by PMV/PPD.

