Appendix L: Research Ethics and Data Governance

L.1 Ethical Principles

Research involving human participants (surveys, deliberative workshops, pilot projects) is governed by:

1. Informed consent. All participants must understand the purpose, methods, and implications of the research.
2. Anonymity. Individual responses are anonymised; only aggregate data is published.
3. Right to withdraw. Participants may withdraw at any time without penalty.
4. Benefit-sharing. Communities involved in research receive feedback and, where appropriate, material benefits.
5. Non-maleficence. Research must not harm participants or communities.

L.2 Data Governance

Data collected through sensors, surveys, and digital platforms is governed by:

1. Data minimisation. Only data necessary for the research is collected.
2. Purpose limitation. Data is used only for the purposes for which it was collected.
3. Storage limitation. Data is retained only as long as necessary.
4. Security. Data is stored securely and access is restricted.
5. Transparency. Data subjects are informed of how their data is used.

L.3 AI Ethics

AI systems in the civic city are governed by:

1. Human oversight. All AI decisions affecting public resources are subject to human review.
2. Explainability. AI decisions are explainable in terms understandable to affected communities.
3. Contestability. Affected communities can contest AI decisions through established procedures.
4. Non-discrimination. AI systems are audited for bias and discrimination.
5. Dignity. AI systems may not violate dignity thresholds under any circumstances.

